LEAD = {
    "headline": "CREATOR BUDGETS REACH 44 BILLION DOLLARS THIS YEAR, 36 PERCENT OF THE PEOPLE SPENDING IT SAY THEY CANNOT SEE HOW MUCH ACTUALLY REACHES THE CREATOR, AND 74 PERCENT AGREE THAT IS BECAUSE SOMEBODY IN THE MIDDLE IS PAID TO KEEP IT THAT WAY",
    "deck": "The advertising industry's own trade body surveyed 53 senior people who run creator marketing and published the answers yesterday.  They say the money has arrived and the plumbing has not.  The sharpest finding is not the 44 billion dollars.  It is that a majority of the people inside the business think the confusion is somebody's business model.",
    "stamps": [
        ("IAB  10 NOV", "https://www.iab.com/news/iab-launches-trusted-creator-brand-deal-initiative/"),
        ("PR NEWSWIRE  10 NOV", "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/iab-launches-trusted-creator-brand-deal-initiative-to-build-the-infrastructure-behind-the-creator-economys-next-phase-of-growth-302934429.html"),
        ("NET INFLUENCER  11 NOV", "https://www.netinfluencer.com/measurement-is-creator-marketing-biggest-problem-survey-finds/"),
        ("IAB REPORT PAGE", "https://www.iab.com/insights/the-creator-economys-next-chapter/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "The Interactive Advertising Bureau is the trade body that writes the rules American digital advertising runs on.  Yesterday it published the first research from its Creator Economy Board, a survey of <mark>53 industry leaders</mark> fielded between July and September, and launched a programme called the Trusted Creator Brand Deal Initiative to fix what the survey found.  The headline number is the size of the prize.  Creator media budgets are expected to reach <mark>44 billion dollars this year</mark>, up from <mark>13.9 billion in 2021</mark>.",
        "Now the uncomfortable part.  Asked what is not working, <mark>67 percent named measurement and attribution</mark>, which is nearly twice as many as named any other problem.  <mark>66 percent</mark> said the single thing they most want is a shared definition of what a view is, so that a view on one platform means roughly the same as a view on another.  On confidence in their own numbers, platform respondents and the creator marketing agencies were the most confident groups, at 67 and 56 percent.  Not one brand or talent management respondent said they were very confident.",
        "Then the money trail.  <mark>36 percent said they have no visibility into how much of the spend actually reaches the creator</mark>, and that group includes eight of the ten platforms surveyed.  <mark>74 percent agreed that fee opacity persists partly because some parts of the industry benefit from it</mark>.  Asked to rate how transparent the whole thing is, the median answer was <mark>5 out of 10</mark>.  Four of the five brand respondents put a fee disclosure standard near the top of their wish list.  One of the ten agencies did.",
        "The mechanism is in a single quote from an agency respondent, and it explains something you have probably watched happen without being able to name it.  \"When client leadership judges creator spend on last-click attribution models built for paid media, the channel gets budget-capped regardless of actual performance.\"  Last-click means the credit for a sale goes entirely to the final thing somebody tapped before buying.  Creator video almost never is that thing.  It is the video somebody watched on Tuesday that made them search your name on Saturday.  Run it through a last-click model and it reads as a flat line, so finance caps it, and the cap has nothing to do with whether the work sold anything.",
        "Two more findings that explain why nobody fixes this internally.  <mark>80 percent agreed</mark> that the team buying media and the team buying creators sit apart and operate independently.  <mark>94 percent</mark> said senior leadership decisions conflict with what the practitioners recommend at least sometimes, 57 percent said frequently, and for 90 percent of them the fight is about budget.  So the people who can see that the measurement is wrong are not the people who set the number.",
        "IAB says the next step is working with the Media Rating Council, the body that audits how advertising gets counted in the United States, to agree common definitions of views, impressions, reach and watch time, with a creator advisory group on its 2027 schedule.  That is the right fix and it is also at least a year out.  Which means for the next twelve months the definition that governs your contract is whichever one you write down first.",
    ],
    "numbers": [("$44B", "creator budgets expected this year"), ("67%", "say measurement is what is broken"), ("36%", "cannot see what reaches the creator")],
    "flagnote": "Read this as the industry's self-diagnosis, not as measurement.  The sample is 53 people and IAB itself calls the findings directional.  Only five of the 53 were brands, so the brand-versus-agency splits in this item are four-out-of-five and one-out-of-ten counts rather than percentages, and we have written them that way deliberately.  Every figure here is an opinion, not a measured campaign outcome.  IAB is also the body publishing the standards programme that these findings justify, and it will convene the measurement work it is calling for.  A study finding that measurement is broken, published by the organisation that will supply the fix, deserves to be read with that in view.",
    "so_what": "The number that caps your creator budget is not a performance number, it is a plumbing number.  Creator video gets judged by a model that can only see the last tap before a purchase, and creator video is almost never the last tap, so it reports as weak no matter how well it worked.  On top of that, a third of the people writing the cheques cannot see what share of the cheque reaches the person who made the video.  Those two problems compound.  You cannot argue for more money with a metric that structurally understates you, and you cannot argue about price when you do not know the split.",
    "do_this": "Write your own definitions into your next creator contract before you discuss money.  Specify in plain words which number counts as a view, who reports it, and from which screen; require the fee split between the agency, any intermediary and the creator in writing; and agree one assisted measure, such as branded search lift or a creator-specific landing page, that the deal will actually be judged on.  Cite the IAB report when you ask, because it is now the industry's own stated direction.",
}

